+## Array search registry
+
+Only std_lower_bound_search existed before Phase 3; it remains unchanged as the
+reference adapter. The independent searchers registry adds linear_search,
+binary_search and exponential_search with optional<size_t> first-match results.
+Absent keys return nullopt. No index structure or allocation is built per lookup;
+all three use O(1) auxiliary space and preserve the caller's data.
+
+| Algorithm | Precondition | Best time | Average time | Worst time |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| linear_search | Any order | O(1) at first key | O(N) for uniformly positioned unique hits; O(N) for misses | O(N) |
+| binary_search | Ascending sorted | O(log N) for this lower-bound implementation on nonempty arrays | O(log N) | O(log N) |
+| exponential_search | Ascending sorted | O(1) at/below first key | O(log N) for uniformly positioned unique hits | O(log N) |
+
+Empty lookups are O(1). Exponential expansion followed by lower-bound refinement
+costs O(log(i+2)) for first-match index i; duplicates return their first occurrence.
+Expansion clamps before doubling to avoid size_t overflow, and refinement uses
+first+(last-first)/2. Binary lower-bound refinement finds the first equal value,
+then checks equality; it does not stop at an arbitrary duplicate. Sorted-input
+preconditions are documented on the public API, with no O(N) check per lookup.
+The search benchmark validates sortedness once outside timing and rejects unsorted
+inputs. Linear's first match refers to original order when used on unsorted data.
+Average-case statements require the stated target model; duplicates and query
+policies change observed costs. Sorting preprocessing is separate from lookup.
+See [search_batch_v1](SEARCH_PROTOCOL_v1.md) for batches and duplicate rank metadata.
